TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R13","met":"partial","where":"Executive Summary; Introduction; Mission Design; Conclusion; Bibliography"},{"id":"R14","met":"no","where":"Images 1–5; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report describes the descending ground-track path as constantly sunlit and suggests a constant supply of sunlight. A sun-synchronous orbit does not guarantee continuous sunlight; eclipse conditions depend on orbital geometry and season. No local time of ascending node is specified to establish the imaging illumination conditions."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The statement that the initial RAAN indicates the location of the launch facility is incorrect. RAAN describes the orientation of the orbital plane at an epoch; it is not the launch-site location. The initial orbital state also lacks an explicit epoch and reference frame."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The mission does not quantify sensor ground resolution, spectral capability, imaging swath, or the ability to detect bleaching through water. The 600 km altitude selection therefore is not demonstrated to meet the proposed fluid-lensing and reef-imaging needs."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The Vega E choice is prospective and has no demonstrated rideshare availability, payload mass compatibility, or confirmed mission matching. The report does not establish that a suitable launch opportunity would inject the spacecraft into the stated orbit."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mount Pleasant is identified as a radio observatory, but the report does not establish its suitability as a spacecraft TT&C ground station. No communications band, antenna/link budget, data rate, or ground-control capability is specified."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access table displays a duplicated 603.382-second record, while the stated total duration and mean correspond to only 15 records rather than all displayed rows. This makes the reported access statistics ambiguous."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The drag sail is proposed without a device size, deployment reliability basis, or estimated orbital decay and re-entry time. The report therefore does not demonstrate that the EOL plan will achieve disposal within an appropriate timeframe."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"The report cites Wikipedia as a source, which the assignment explicitly does not accept as a proper source. Several in-text/table reference numbers also do not appear in the bibliography."}],"overall":"The report presents a coherent Australian reef-monitoring concept and addresses most required mission-design topics with STK output. Its main limitations are unverified payload and ground-station capability, gaps in orbit and EOL justification, inconsistent access statistics, and incomplete referencing and figure labelling.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The mission purpose and need are explained with useful environmental context and sources, and the report gives a reason for using one spacecraft. Likely users are identified, but the mission operator and customer roles are not clearly distinguished, and the concept's operational rationale remains somewhat general.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"The satellite must provide images for a fluid lensing tool to analyse reefs beneath the water surface."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."},{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"The satellite, nicknamed ZYZZsat (Z-axis/Y-axis Zeroed Zoning Satellite), is operated and accessed from Australia ground facilities"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Most required design topics are addressed, and STK imagery and access outputs provide some evidence. However, key design choices and performance are not adequately supported: payload imaging capability, launch compatibility, communication links, and EOL disposal lifetime are not quantified. The access table also contains a duplicate entry and its summary statistics do not account for all displayed rows.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion recaps the selected launch vehicle, orbit, and ground facility and acknowledges launch uncertainty. It is relevant but mainly summarizes the proposal; it does not critically draw conclusions from the design evidence or qualify unsupported claims about illumination and ground-station performance.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"The Mt Pleasant Radio Observatory is well-located to receive data from this orbit type constantly and for long durations."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a clear overall structure, executive summary, and a logical progression through design sections. Presentation is weakened by grammar and incomplete wording, inconsistently numbered or titled figures and tables, and bare or mismatched references. Wikipedia is used as a source despite the assignment's restriction.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"DN path for orbits 1-15"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"It acts like a regular sail, utilising a large surface area to ‘ and is suitable for low-earth orbit altitudes [10]."}]}]}
